 {
  "problemId": "calc-ab-top-005",
  "skillId": "continuity-types",
  "prompt": "The function f(x) = (x² − 9)/(x − 3) has what kind of discontinuity at x = 3?",
  "svg": null,
  "answer": {
   "type": "exact",
   "value": "Removable (a hole)",
   "equivalents": [
    "Removable(ahole)"
   ]
  },
  "answerType": "multiple-choice",
  "options": [
   {
    "label": "A",
    "text": "Removable (a hole)"
   },
   {
    "label": "B",
    "text": "Jump"
   },
   {
    "label": "C",
    "text": "Infinite (vertical asymptote)"
   },
   {
    "label": "D",
    "text": "None — it is continuous"
   }
  ],
  "correctOption": "A",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "gradeBand": "Calculus",
  "explanation": "Factoring gives (x − 3)(x + 3)/(x − 3) = x + 3 for x ≠ 3, so the limit exists and equals 6 — the graph has a single missing point. A discontinuity whose two-sided limit EXISTS is removable. Choice C is tempting because the denominator hits zero, but nothing blows up here: the (x − 3) cancels.",
